    9| # ===========================================================================
   10| # nn/__init__.bend -- tinygrad/nn/__init__.py (420 lines).
   11| #
   12| # FIRST, THE FINDING THAT CHANGES THE SHAPE OF THIS FILE: THERE IS NO
   13| # `nn.Module`. I went looking for one and it is not there -- `nn/__init__.py` is
   14| # eleven plain classes with no base class, no `parameters()`, no `state_dict()`
   15| # method and no registry, and `get_state_dict` (state.py:89) reaches a layer by
   16| # walking `obj.__dict__` by REFLECTION. So the "Module base" the hand-off asked
   17| # for is not a thing to port; it is `state.bend`'s `Obj` tree, and this file's
   18| # job is to be the thing that tree is built from. That is why this file imports
   19| # `state.bend` and not the other way round, which is also Python's own direction
   20| # (`nn/__init__.py:6`, `from tinygrad.nn import optim, state, datasets`).
   21| #
   22| # WHY A LAYER IS NOT A `Data` RECORD HERE, and it is the second real finding. In
   23| # Python a layer is an OBJECT: its parameters are attributes, so `get_state_dict`
   24| # finds them by reflection without the layer cooperating, and
   25| # `self.eps: float`/`self.bias: Tensor|None` are just attributes. In Bend a
   26| # `Data` record has NO REFLECTION and a `Data` field cannot hold a `Maybe`
   27| # (measured: bend2-constraints rule 15), so every field read would need a paired
   28| # accessor def (`Tensor.ar`/`Tensor.u` in tensor.bend is that pattern) and
   29| # `Tensor|None` would need a third type. So each layer here is a def over its
   30| # CONSTRUCTOR ARGUMENTS that answers a SHAPE, plus a `*_st` def that answers the
