### tinybendygrad/uop/weak.bend  (1844 lines)
    1| # weak.bend -- tinygrad/uop/weak.py: the four `PatternMatcher` tables that commit
    2| # weak dtypes at lowering boundaries. 5 of weak.py's 9 defs and 3 of its 8 rules.
    3| #
    4| # TWO DEPENDENCIES, measured. `dtype.bend` is unreachable to any importer (it prints a
    5| # foreign-code notice for `Dt.bf16`, `Dt.fp16`, the `fp8_*` and the `i64_*`/`float_to_*`
    6| # helpers), so `strong_dtype` and `weak_dtype` are spelled HERE, as `fold.bend` did for
    7| # `least_upper_dtype`. `least_upper_dtype` is `F.promo_dtype`. `dtype_from_uop` over a
    8| # chosen src list is NOT a wall: `F.dt_shape` is `dtype_from_uop` with its src half
    9| # factored out as `List<Derived>`, and `Derived` is `Data`, so a fold rebuilds one per
   10| # hypothetical src (`wk_derived`/`wk_dtype_at` below). Asking the fold about a node that
   11| # does not exist would need a scratch arena and a second Kahn worklist per query.
   12| #
   13| # `UOp._min_max` (ops.py:1104-1158) IS LANDED, and the reader that joins it to
   14| # `commit_dtype` is landed with it. `fold.bend:2176-2184` once recorded the sweep as the
   15| # biggest single piece left in that half of ops.py and named what was missing (`i64_mul`,
   16| # `i64_div`, `i64_mod`; there is no F32 min/max) -- all of which now exist, and the
   17| # arithmetic core is at `fold.bend:2648` under a "when helpers.bend grows them these defs
   18| # go there" notice that has been kept.
   19| #
   20| # SO THE NINE MARKERS BELOW THAT SAY "`commit_dtype` IS MISSING" ARE OUT OF DATE, and the
   21| # wall was never a LANGUAGE limit. It was an IMPORT TOPOLOGY: the arithmetic is
   22| # `D.commit_dtype` in `mixin/dtype.bend`, which does not import this file, and `wk_dt` is
   23| # here -- so one call could not be written. `wk_commit_dtype` is that call, and
   24| # `.agents/slop/wk-cd-gate.sh` is its gate: 7 rows, three lanes, 6 identical and ONE
   25| # DOCUMENTED DIVERGENCE (`cd_none`, a PARAM with no `vmin_vmax`, where the sweep has no
   26| # interval to report and CPython answers `i64`). The nine markers are now blocked on their
   27| # own RULE BODIES and not on a reader.
   28| #
   29| # WHAT THE GATE HAS TO CONTAIN, because the obvious fixture proves nothing: a PARAM whose
   30| # dtype is int32 answers `i32` on EVERY row, because the weakint branch is not taken. And
  ... cited lines ...
